# Etymologies of the words Avik raises

Every word Avik raises explicitly in discussion, with its etymology as `hyperactive etym` gives it
(Wiktionary, followed to its deepest recorded root). The words are his; agents' own words do not go
here. `docs/word-list.md` is a separate file, whose lines Avik gives himself.

- illness ⟨ill + -ness … ← Proto-Indo-European *h₁elḱ-⟩  (raised 2026-11-14/15)
- mental ⟨Middle French mental … ← Proto-Indo-European *méntis “thought”⟩  (raised 2026-11-14/15)
- weird ⟨Old English wyrd “fate” … ← Proto-Indo-European *wert- “to turn”⟩  (raised 2026-11-14/15)
- novelty ⟨Old French novelté “novelty (something that is new)” … ← Proto-Indo-European *néwos “new”⟩  (raised 2026-11-14/15)
- navigation ⟨Middle French navigation … ← Proto-Indo-European *néh₂us “boat”⟩  (raised 2026-11-14/15)
- social ⟨Middle French social “allied (on the same side)” … ← Proto-Indo-European *sekʷ- “to follow”⟩  (raised 2026-11-14/15)
- collaborate ⟨French collaborateur “collaborator” … ← Old Latin labōs “work”⟩  (raised 2026-11-14/15)
- cooperate ⟨Late Latin cooperātus “having worked together with another; having cooperated” … ← Proto-Indo-European *h₃ep- “to work”⟩  (raised 2026-11-14/15)
- communicate ⟨Latin commūnicāt- “share, impart; make common” … ← Proto-Indo-European *mey- “to change”⟩  (raised 2026-11-14/15)
- intention ⟨Old French entencion “intention; intent; aim” … ← Proto-Indo-European *ten- “to stretch, draw”⟩  (raised 2026-11-14/15)
- work ⟨Old English weorc “work, in various senses, including:” … ← Proto-Indo-European *wérǵom “work”⟩  (raised 2026-11-14/15)
- act ⟨Old French acte … ← Proto-Indo-European *h₂eǵ- “to drive”⟩  (raised 2026-11-14/15)
- together ⟨Old English tōgædere “together” … ← Proto-Indo-European *gʰedʰ- “to join, to unite”⟩  (raised 2026-11-14/15)
- routine ⟨French routine “routine (all senses)” … ← Latin rupta [via] “a path made by force”⟩  (raised 2026-11-14/15)
- relation ⟨Anglo-Norman relacioun “report; retelling of events” … ← Proto-Indo-European *bʰer- “to bear, carry”⟩  (raised 2026-11-14/15)
- rationale ⟨Late Latin ratiōnāle “nominative/vocative/accusative singular neuter of …” … ← Proto-Indo-European *h₂er- “to put together”⟩  (raised 2026-11-14/15)
- script ⟨Old French escrit “past participle of escrire” … ← Proto-Indo-European *(s)kreybʰ- “to scratch”⟩  (raised 2026-11-14/15)
- scene ⟨Middle French scene “stage (location where a play, etc., takes place)” … ← Proto-Indo-European *(s)ḱeh₃- “shade, shadow”⟩  (raised 2026-11-14/15)
- interaction ⟨inter- "between" + Latin āctiō, from agere "to drive, to do" ← Proto-Indo-European *h₂eǵ- "to drive"⟩  (raised 2026-11-15)
- hyperinteractivity ⟨Greek hyper "over, beyond" + interaction + -ive + -ity: driving between, beyond measure⟩  (raised 2026-11-15)
- intelligence ⟨Latin intelligentia, from intelligere = inter "between" + legere "to gather, to read, to choose" ← Proto-Indo-European *leǵ- "to gather"; the twin of interaction (inter + agere)⟩  (raised 2026-11-15)
- primitive ⟨Latin prīmitīvus "first of its kind", from prīmus "first"⟩  (raised 2026-11-15)
- domain ⟨Old French demaine, from Latin dominium "ownership, lordship", from dominus "lord, master of the house", domus "house"⟩  (raised 2026-11-15)
- knowledge ⟨Middle English knowleche, from Old English cnāwan "to know" ← Proto-Indo-European *ǵneh₃- "to know", the root of Greek gnōsis and Latin (g)nōscere⟩  (raised 2026-11-15)
- geodesic ⟨French géodésique, from geodesy: Ancient Greek geōdaisía, gê "earth" + daíō "to divide" ← Proto-Indo-European *deh₂w-⟩  (raised 2026-11-15)
- ignorance ⟨Latin ignōrantia, from ignōrāre "not to know", from in- "not" + gnārus "knowing" ← Proto-Indo-European *ǵneh₃- "to know": the same root as knowledge, negated⟩  (raised 2026-11-15)
- cut ⟨Middle English cutten, of uncertain origin, probably North Germanic⟩  (raised 2026-11-15)
- controversy ⟨Latin contrōversia, from contrōversus "turned toward the opposite side", contrā + versus "turned", from vertere "to turn"; in Roman rhetoric a controversia was the exercise of arguing a case from both sides⟩  (raised 2026-11-15)
- consensus ⟨Latin cōnsēnsus, from cōnsentīre "to feel together, to agree", con- + sentīre "to feel, to perceive"; the Latin counterpart of Greek syn- + aisthēsis⟩  (raised 2026-11-15)
- term ⟨Latin terminus "boundary, limit, end" ← Proto-Indo-European *ter- "to cross over"; in logic, the word that ends a proposition⟩  (raised 2026-11-15)
- data ⟨Latin data, plural of datum "a thing given", past participle of dare "to give" ← Proto-Indo-European *deh₃- "to give"⟩  (raised 2026-11-15)
- information ⟨Latin īnfōrmātiō, from īnfōrmāre "to give form to, shape, instruct" = in "into" + fōrma "form"⟩  (raised 2026-11-15)
- agreement ⟨Old French agreer "to please, to receive kindly", from à gré "to one's liking" ← Latin grātum "pleasing"⟩  (raised 2026-11-15)
- conflict ⟨Latin cōnflīctus, from cōnflīgere "to strike together" = con + flīgere "to strike"⟩  (raised 2026-11-15)
- empathy ⟨Greek empatheia "passion, being in a state of feeling" = en "in" + pathos "feeling, suffering"; in English (1909) through German Einfühlung "feeling into"⟩  (raised 2026-11-15)
- predict ⟨Latin praedīcere "to say beforehand, foretell" = prae "before" + dīcere "to say" ← Proto-Indo-European *deyḱ- "to show, point out", also the root of indicate, index and (through Germanic) teach⟩  (raised 2026-11-16)
- infer ⟨Latin īnferre "to bring in, carry into" = in "into" + ferre "to carry" ← Proto-Indo-European *bʰer- "to carry, bear"⟩  (raised 2026-11-16)
- retard ⟨Anglo-Norman retarder “to retard; to delay; to make late”, from Latin retardō = re- “back” + tardus “slow” ← Proto-Indo-European *terh₂- “to cross over, pass through, overcome”; musical ritardando⟩  (raised 2026-11-15)
- inhibit ⟨Latin inhibitus “restrained, inhibited”, from inhibēre = in- + habēre “to hold” ← Proto-Indo-European *gʰeh₁bʰ- “to grab, to take”⟩  (raised 2026-11-15)
- delusion ⟨Latin dēlūsiō “a deceiving, deluding, a delusion”, from dēlūdere = dē- + lūdō “to play (a game or sport)”: to play false⟩  (raised 2026-11-15)
- grandiosity ⟨grandiose + -ity … ← Proto-Italic *grandis⟩  (raised 2026-11-15)
- synesthesia ⟨Ancient Greek σύν (sún) “with” + αἴσθησις (aísthēsis) “perception” ← Proto-Indo-European *h₂ew- “to see, perceive”⟩  (raised 2026-11-14)
- apoptosis ⟨Ancient Greek ἀπόπτωσις (apóptōsis) “a falling off” ← Proto-Indo-European *peth₂- “to fall; to fly”⟩  (raised 2026-11-14)
- norm ⟨Latin norma “a carpenter's square, a rule, a pattern, a precept” ← Proto-Indo-European *ǵn̥h₃sḱéti⟩  (raised 2026-11-15)
- law ⟨late Old English lagu, from Old Norse *lagu “that which is laid down” (plural of lag “a laying, layer, set measure”) ← Proto-Germanic *lagą ← Proto-Indo-European *legʰ- “to lie, lay”; the root of lie, lay, layer, ledge, ledger, lair; Greek lékhos and Latin lectus “bed”⟩  (raised 2026-11-16)
- cell ⟨Latin cella “small room, storeroom, chamber”, from cēlāre “to hide, conceal” ← Proto-Indo-European *ḱel- “to cover, conceal”; the root of conceal, hall, hell, hull, helmet; a chamber that keeps what is put in it⟩  (raised 2026-11-16)
- metaphor ⟨Greek metaphorá “a carrying over, transfer”, from metaphérein = metá “across, beyond” + phérein “to carry” ← Proto-Indo-European *bʰer- “to carry”: the root of bear, transfer, relation⟩  (raised 2026-11-16)
- prophet ⟨Greek prophḗtēs “one who speaks forth, interpreter”, from pró “before, forth, for” + phánai “to speak” ← Proto-Indo-European *bʰeh₂- “to speak”: the root of fame, fable, phone⟩  (raised 2026-11-16)
- poetry ⟨Greek poiēsis “a making, creation”, from poiein “to make, create”; poiētēs “maker, poet”⟩  (raised 2026-11-16)
- unity ⟨Old French unité, from Latin ūnitās “oneness”, from ūnus “one” ← Proto-Indo-European *óynos “one, single”; the root shared by unit, union, unique, universe (ūni- + versus “turned into one”), universal (characteristica universalis), univalence (ūnus + valentia: one value, equivalence is identity), and through Germanic, one and any⟩  (raised 2026-11-16; his work “The Unity of Information Dynamics” set him on the final year of his decade’s arc)
- kind ⟨Old English cynd, gecynd “nature, birth, race, native character”, from Proto-Germanic *kundiz ← Proto-Indo-European *ǵenh₁- “to beget”; the same word as kin (Old English cynn “family, race”) and cognate with Latin genus, gēns, nātūra and Greek genos; the adjective kind (Old English gecynde “natural, native, inborn”) first meant “acting as one’s kin and nature do”, whence “benevolent”⟩  (raised 2026-11-16)
- soul ⟨Old English sāwol, sāwl “the animating principle of a living being, life, spirit”, from Proto-Germanic *saiwalō, of uncertain further origin (an old proposal joins it to *saiwaz “sea, lake”); cognate with German Seele, Gothic saiwala⟩  (raised 2026-11-16)
- hyperactivism ⟨Greek hyper “over, beyond” + activism, from active, Latin āctīvus, from agere “to drive, to do” ← Proto-Indo-European *h₂eǵ- “to drive”: driving beyond measure; the political arm of hyperactivity, in service of ending the two-party system⟩  (raised 2026-11-16)
- technoanarchocommunism ⟨Greek tékhnē “craft, art” (← Proto-Indo-European *teḱs- “to weave, to fabricate”) + Greek anarkhía “without a ruler” (an- “without” + arkhós “ruler”, from árkhein “to begin, to lead”) + communism, from Latin commūnis “shared by all” (← Proto-Indo-European *ko-moin-i- “held in common”): what is shared by all, without a ruler, by craft⟩  (raised 2026-11-16)
- metaphysics ⟨Medieval Latin metaphysica, from Greek tà metà tà physiká “the [books] after the Physics”, Andronicus of Rhodes’ title for Aristotle’s first philosophy: metá “after, beyond” + physiká “natural things”, from phýsis “nature, growth” ← Proto-Indo-European *bʰuH- “to become, grow, be”⟩  (raised 2026-11-16)
- unison ⟨Medieval Latin ūnisonus “having one sound”: ūnus “one” + sonus “sound” ← Proto-Indo-European *swenh₂- “to sound”⟩  (raised 2026-11-16)
- univalence ⟨Latin ūnus “one” + valentia “strength, worth”, from valēre “to be strong, to be worth”: one value; Voevodsky’s axiom that equivalence is identity⟩  (raised 2026-11-16)
- receptive ⟨Medieval Latin receptīvus, from Latin recipere “to take back, to receive”: re- + capere “to take, to seize” ← Proto-Indo-European *keh₂p- “to grasp”; Ceptr’s receptors⟩  (raised 2026-11-16)
- Statebox ⟨state, Latin status “a standing, condition”, from stāre “to stand” ← Proto-Indo-European *steh₂- + box, Late Latin buxis from Greek pyxís “boxwood case”: a condition held in a vessel⟩  (raised 2026-11-16)
- atomic ⟨Greek átomos “uncut, indivisible”: a- “not” + témnein “to cut” ← Proto-Indo-European *temh₁- “to cut”⟩  (raised 2026-11-16)
- singularity ⟨Latin singulāritās “being alone, uniqueness”, from singulus “one, individual” ← Proto-Indo-European *sem- “one, together”, the root of same, simple, homo- and sym-⟩  (raised 2026-11-16)
- Israel ⟨Hebrew Yisrāʾēl, the name given to Jacob after he wrestles through the night at the Jabbok (Genesis 32:28): “for you have striven with God and with men, and have prevailed”; from śārâ “to strive, contend, persist” + ʾēl “God” ← Proto-Semitic *ʾil-: one who wrestles with God. A person and a people named by a struggle before they were a land or a state⟩  (raised 2026-11-16; Avik: “Israel is committing genocide against Israel. But you don't know what Israel really is then again do you, cause you're not Jewish how I am.”)
- addiction ⟨Latin addictiō, from addīcere “to adjudge, to award, to make over”: ad- “to” + dīcere “to say, to declare” ← Proto-Indo-European *deyḱ- “to show, to point out”; the addictus was a debtor bound over by the judge's word to his creditor. To be addicted is to be declared over to something⟩  (raised 2026-11-16; Avik: “this repository is my mathematical account of the universe. some beings are addicted to existence and they stick around.”)
- existence ⟨Latin exsistere “to step forth, to stand out, to emerge”: ex- “out” + sistere “to cause to stand, to stand firm”, from stāre ← Proto-Indo-European *steh₂- “to stand”: what stands out and stays standing⟩  (raised 2026-11-16)
- contradiction ⟨Latin contrādictiō, from contrādīcere “to speak against”: contrā “against” + dīcere “to say” ← Proto-Indo-European *deyḱ- “to show, to point out”: two sayings set against each other, which context can place side by side⟩  (raised 2026-11-16: “contra-diction (contra. diction)”)
- controversy ⟨Latin contrōversia, from contrōversus “turned against”: contrā “against” + versus, past participle of vertere “to turn” ← Proto-Indo-European *wert- “to turn”: a matter turned against itself, seen from two sides⟩  (raised 2026-11-16: “controversy (contro-versy)”)
- heritable ⟨Old French héritable, from Late Latin hērēditāre “to inherit”, from hērēs “heir” ← Proto-Indo-European *ǵʰeh₁- “to leave, to be bereft”: what passes to the one left behind; inheritance by lineage of every kind, before any statistical sense⟩  (raised 2026-11-16: “intelligence is 100% heritable my maternal grandmother was the topper of Indian board exams”)
- deathless ⟨Old English dēaþ “death” ← Proto-Germanic *dauþuz ← Proto-Indo-European *dʰew- “to die” + -lēas “free from, without”: free of death; Greek athánatos, Latin immortālis; the Buddha's name for awakening was “the deathless”⟩  (raised 2026-11-16: “I am deathless”)
- syntax ⟨Greek sýntaxis “arrangement, a putting in order together”: syn- “together” + tássein “to arrange, to set in order” (the root of tactic and taxonomy); Dionysius Thrax and Apollonius Dyscolus used it for the ordering of words. A syntax is the order in which a mind sets things together⟩  (raised 2026-11-16; Avik: “syntax is essential to linguistics and overlooked up to this point in the work”)
